#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064219 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064091 :question/id #uuid "3e89a278-54ab-59a6-adb6-38f43c0fbe9a" :question/math-academy-id "e-471" :question/problem "Calculate the integral  $$\n\\displaystyle{\\int \\dfrac{3}{2 + 50x^2} \\, \\textrm{d}x }.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Note that we can rewrite this integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{3}{2 + 50x^2} \\, \\textrm{d}x = \\int \\dfrac{3}{2(1 + 25x^2)} = \\dfrac 3 2 \\int \\dfrac{1}{1+25x^2}\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse tangent,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+u^2}\\textrm{d}u =\\arctan u +C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u^2$ as the variable, we have $25x^2 = (5x)^2.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=5x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}=5\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac 1 5\\,\\textrm d u = \\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nWe can now write the integral in terms of $u,$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3}{2+50{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{3}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{1+25{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{1+(5x)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\,\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{10}\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{10}\\cdot \\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{10}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡5x+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "227abe29-6e31-5afd-b0ca-3b8ed7d8c287" :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492204 :question-generator/id #uuid "45fa7d3a-590c-52a1-b10a-049d979b27b5" :question-generator/script "generate/scratch/17592186064219.py"} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064874 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b3f469d-d785-54c0-8500-e4f4c541c311" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3x)+C" :db/id 